Here's how to implement all three requirements:

## 1. Custom "Cancel" Back Button (on EditProductPage)

```xml
<!-- EditProductPage.xaml -->
<ContentPage ...>
    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior TextOverride="Cancel"
                           Command="{Binding BackCommand}" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

```csharp
// In your ViewModel
[RelayCommand]
async Task Back()
{
    if (HasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        bool discard = await Shell.Current.DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "Discard changes and go back?",
            "Discard", "Stay");
        if (!discard) return;
    }
    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
}
```

## 2. Guard the Hardware/Gesture Back Navigation

The custom back button command handles taps on "Cancel", but you also need to intercept the system back (swipe gesture, hardware button). Do this in `AppShell.xaml.cs`:

```csharp
protected override async void OnNavigating(ShellNavigatingEventArgs args)
{
    base.OnNavigating(args);

    if (args.Source == ShellNavigationSource.Pop
        && Shell.Current.CurrentPage is EditProductPage page
        && page.BindingContext is EditProductViewModel vm
        && vm.HasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        var deferral = args.GetDeferral();

        bool discard = await Shell.Current.DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "Discard changes and go back?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (!discard)
            args.Cancel();

        deferral.Complete();
    }
}
```

## Key Points

- **`BackButtonBehavior`** replaces the default arrow with "Cancel" and routes taps through your command.
- **`OnNavigating` + `GetDeferral()`** intercepts system-level back navigation (swipe, hardware button) so you can show an async dialog and cancel if needed.
- Always call `deferral.Complete()` whether you cancel or not — otherwise navigation hangs.
